{"schema":"zzx-html-edition-profile-v1","country":"X-CIA-PF","name":"Paracel Islands","year":2007,"status":"source-extracted; field review pending","source":{"name":"factbook-2007.zip","member":"factbook-2007/geos/pf.html","sha256":"001cd13cbbcb758b59f420fb59ade92276d5e8064d48408cd7df0ebcc13d4869","format":"html"},"rights_notice_member":"factbook-2007/docs/contributor_copyright.html","rights_notice_sha256":"0aa0c111c3015a738f223e5be4ed3275a829514a52d1781742a8671e915bdb1e","fields":[{"category":"introduction","label":"Background","content":"The Paracel Islands are surrounded by productive fishing grounds and by potential oil and gas reserves. In 1932, French Indochina annexed the islands and set up a weather station on Pattle Island; maintenance was continued by its successor, Vietnam. China has occupied the Paracel Islands since 1974, when its troops seized a South Vietnamese garrison occupying the western islands. The islands are claimed by Taiwan and Vietnam.","ordinal":1,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/pf.html#field:1","country":"X-CIA-PF","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Location","content":"Southeastern Asia, group of small islands and reefs in the South China Sea, about one-third of the way from central Vietnam to the northern Philippines","ordinal":2,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/pf.html#field:2","country":"X-CIA-PF","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Geographic coordinates","content":"16 30 N, 112 00 E","ordinal":3,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/pf.html#field:3","country":"X-CIA-PF","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Map references","content":"Southeast Asia","ordinal":4,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/pf.html#field:4","country":"X-CIA-PF","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Area","content":"total: NA sq km\n\nland: NA sq km\n\nwater: 0 sq km","ordinal":5,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/pf.html#field:5","country":"X-CIA-PF","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Area - compa